  88:     """
  89:     Extract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.
  90: 
  91:     Input structure:
  92:     - The boundary color is the background/separator.
  93:     - Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.
  94:     - Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.
  95: 
  96:     Rule:
  97:     - Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.
  98:     - Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.
  99:     - Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.
 100: 
 101:     Key insight:
 102:     - The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.
 103:     """
 104:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
 105:     assert g.ndim == 2, "expected a 2D grid"
 106:     bg = _boundary_bg(g)
 107: 
 108:     objects = []
 109:     for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=False):
 110:         bbox = tuple(map(int, comp["bbox"]))
 111:         sub = crop_bbox(g, bbox)
 112:         assert np.all(sub != bg), "foreground rectangles must be dense"
 113: 
 114:         base_color = _unique_most_common(sub)
 115:         marker_colors = set(map(int, sub.reshape(-1))) - {int(base_color)}
 116:         objects.append(
 117:             {
 118:                 "bbox": bbox,
 119:                 "sub": sub,
 120:                 "base_color": int(base_color),
 121:                 "marker_colors": marker_colors,
 122:             }
 123:         )
 124: 
 125:     plain = [obj for obj in objects if not obj["marker_colors"]]
 126:     assert len(plain) == 1, "expected exactly one plain rectangle"
 127:     target = plain[0]
 128:     tr0, tc0, tr1, tc1 = target["bbox"]
 129:     target_shape = (int(tr1 - tr0 + 1), int(tc1 - tc0 + 1))
 130: 
 131:     sources = [
